call_details.response:
The remaining winning lines on the board are extremely limited. All verticals in columns 0–4 are already mixed or blocked, as are all down-left diagonals and most horizontals. This leaves only:

- o can complete a horizontal 4 in row 4 by occupying both (4,5) and (4,6) (i.e., taking the second piece in both empty columns);
- o can complete a horizontal 4 in row 2 by occupying (2,5);
- x can complete a horizontal 4 in row 3 or the down-right diagonal only by occupying both (3,6) and either (3,5) or (2,5);
- x can complete a late horizontal 4 in row 1 by occupying both fifth-row squares of columns 5 and 6.

Columns 5 and 6 are therefore the only relevant files; column 0 is a pure tempo square.

If x opens in column 5 or 6, o immediately takes the second cell of that column. The remaining file then becomes “dangerous”: the first player to enter it allows o to take its second cell and win at once on row 4. After x’s forced tempo in column 0 there remain an odd number of safe squares. Both players are compelled to exhaust those squares, after which x is forced to open the dangerous file and o wins.

Opening in column 0 avoids handing o a second-cell on the first move. o can still start one of the empty files, but x can answer in the same file, taking its second cell and thereby permanently blocking row 4. Thereafter every attempt by o to claim a third- or fourth-row cell in column 5 is either an immediate loss for o (x answers on the diagonal or on row 3) or can be answered by x while still keeping the parity of the remaining squares in x’s favour. The net result is that o can always block x’s last remaining line (row 1) but cannot create a line of his own. Perfect play therefore ends in a draw.

Because the other two legal moves lose against perfect play, column 0 is the unique optimal choice.

